Creating a geocode of an address using Google Maps Javascript is surprisingly easy. In just a few lines of code you can take a physical address, like '123 Main Street', and turn it into geographic coordinates that can be used to place markers on a map. This tutorial will give you step-by-step instructions on how to geocode an address with Google Maps Javascript.

First, create a basic web page that contains a Google Map Javascript API. This is contained within the code that you write and provides the initial map displayed on the page.

Next, create a text box that the user can enter the street address into. This will need to be within the HTML document as well.

Additionally, create a button input for the user to click and geocode the address. This is then bound to the onclick event.

To start off, declare a variable outside of the initialize function. This will store the class object that is used to store the geocode.

Within the initialize() function (the one place in the code where this is possible) set the geocoder variable to an instance of the Google Maps geocoder class. This can be done by creating a new object with the following line:

var geocoder = new google.maps.Geocoder();

Then add a codeAddress() function to the javascript code. This is the function that will is used to geocode the address.

The first line of the codeAddress function should be used to get the address from the text box and then place it into a variable. This can be done using the getElementById method.

The next step is to use the geocode() method of the geocoder object and pass in two parameters. The first is the address (in this case, the variable holding the contents of the text box we had set up earlier) and the second is a callback function.

The callback function should first check the status value that is returned. This status value tells us if the geocode was successful. If it is, the status will be equal to "OK".

If the status does equal "OK" then we can call the setCenter() method of the map object variable. This will set the center of the map to the coordinates found by the geocode operation.

Similarly, we can also use the same result geometry location to add a map marker to the map object variable. This is done using the google.maps.Marker() method and the necessary parameters.

Finally, we're going to add an alert message to the ELSE to let the user know that the Geocode didn't work like it should have. This can be done using the alert() method.

Your code should now look something like this:

var geocoder;

function initialize() {
  var mapOptions = {
    center: new google.maps.LatLng(-34.397, 150.644),
    zoom: 8
  };
  var map = new google.maps.Map(
    document.getElementById("map_canvas"),
    mapOptions
  );
  geocoder = new google.maps.Geocoder();
}

function codeAddress() {
  var sAddress = document.getElementById("address").value;
  geocoder.geocode({ address: sAddress }, function(results, status) {
    if (status == google.maps.GeocoderStatus.OK) {
      map.setCenter(results[0].geometry.location);
      var marker = new google.maps.Marker({
        map: map,
        position: results[0].geometry.location
      });
    } else {
      alert("Geocode was not successful for the following reason: " + status);
    }
  });
}

Once everything is in place you can give it a go! To do so, enter an address into the text box and press the 'geocode' button to see the associated Google Map with your desired location. With just a few lines of code, you can quickly and easily geocode an address with Google Maps Javascript.

